import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const reqCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Elicit: ask the real users', note: 'Requirements come from people who rarely say what they mean. Watch the work, not just the wish list.' },
    { math: '2 · Separate functional from non-functional', note: '"The system sends invoices" is functional. "Invoices send in under a second, encrypted" is non-functional — and usually the harder promise.' },
    { math: '3 · Make them testable', note: '"Fast" is a wish; "p95 under 300 ms" is a requirement. If you cannot write the test, you have not written the requirement.' },
    { math: '4 · Specify, then validate', note: 'Write them down (stories, use cases, specs), then walk them back past the stakeholders — cheap now, ruinous later.' },
    { math: '5 · Expect change', note: 'Requirements are living artefacts. The engineering is not freezing them; it is managing their change without lying to anyone.' },
  ],
};

const costCfg = {
  points: [
    { points: [[1, 1], [2, 3], [3, 8], [4, 16], [5, 30], [6, 60]], color: '#b42318', label: 'relative cost to fix a requirements defect', mode: 'line' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.5, max: 6.5, label: 'phase found: 1 requirements → 6 production' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 70, label: 'relative cost (×)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/software-engineering/foundational/software-requirements">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Software projects rarely die at the keyboard. They die in the months before anyone
    codes — in the meetings where "what we should build" was settled by whoever spoke last,
    in the requirement nobody wrote down because it seemed obvious, in the stakeholder who
    was never asked. Requirements engineering is the discipline that stands between an idea
    and a system: working out what to build, writing it down in a form that can be tested,
    and managing the certainty that the answer will change. It is the least glamorous phase
    of software engineering and the one with the best claim to decide everything else.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <em>requirement</em> is a statement of what the system must do or how well it must do
    it, precise enough that everyone — client, designer, tester — would agree on whether the
    finished system satisfies it. That precision bar is what separates requirements from
    wishes. "The app should be fast" is a wish; "search results return within 300
    milliseconds at the 95th percentile with 10,000 concurrent users" is a requirement,
    because you can write the test that fails. This testability rule sounds bureaucratic
    until you watch a project without it: two teams, each sincerely believing the other
    agreed to something different, discovering the disagreement at acceptance testing — the
    most expensive possible moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline has an uncomfortable secret: it is not really about writing documents.
    It is about discovering what people need — which they often cannot articulate, sometimes
    do not know, and occasionally will not say — and converting that into artefacts that
    survive contact with construction. The chapter follows that arc: elicitation (finding
    the truth), classification (functional versus non-functional), and specification and
    validation (writing it down and proving it right, before the cost curve punishes you).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from elicitation to living specification" widget="equationStep" config={reqCfg} />

  <h2>Elicitation and stakeholders</h2>
  <p>
    Requirements come from <em>stakeholders</em> — and the first skill is realising how many
    of them there are, and how few of them speak in the kickoff meeting. The person who pays
    for the software, the person who uses it daily, the person who maintains it at 3 a.m.,
    the regulator who audits it and the customer-service agent who absorbs its failures are
    five different people with five different definitions of success. Missing any of them
    costs later: systems that delight managers and enrage operators are a genre, not an
    accident.
  </p>
  <p>
    Elicitation is the craft of drawing requirements out, and no single technique suffices.
    Interviews get you stated needs; <em>observation</em> gets you actual ones — watching
    nurses chart medication reveals workarounds, sticky notes and shadow spreadsheets that
    no interview would surface, each one an unmet requirement wearing a disguise. Workshops
    force conflicting stakeholders into the same room where their conflicts become visible
    early; prototypes and walk-throughs exploit the fact that people react to something
    concrete far more reliably than they speculate about something abstract. A classic trap
    deserves its name: the marketing requirement. "Users want more features" usually means
    "the loudest stakeholder wants more features" — real users often want less software that
    fits the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Elicitation is also where requirements conflicts surface, and surfacing them early is
    the point. A hospital booking system gives a compact example: clinicians want full
    details visible in one click (speed of care), administrators want access gated by role
    (privacy compliance), patients want their own data visible (transparency). All three are
    legitimate; some cannot be fully satisfied simultaneously; and the negotiation that
    resolves them — read-only summary views, audit logs, granular consent — is requirements
    engineering, not politics. A specification that hides the conflict simply schedules the
    same argument for a later, more expensive date.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because not every elicited wish can be built, elicitation flows directly into
    <em>prioritisation</em>. The MoSCoW scheme is the workhorse: Must (the release fails
    without it), Should (important but deferrable), Could (nice if cheap), Won't (not this
    time — and we are saying so on the record). The discipline lives in the fourth
    category: a written, agreed statement of what is out of scope is the cheapest
    conflict-prevention device in the field. Prioritisation also disciplines the stakeholder
    map itself, because must-have lists signed by people who will not fund them have a way
    of rewriting themselves at integration time — and that negotiation is cheaper in a
    workshop than in a change request.
  </p>

  <h2>Functional versus non-functional requirements</h2>
  <p>
    The classification everyone must master splits requirements by what kind of claim they
    make. <em>Functional</em> requirements state what the system does: process a refund,
    reject duplicate invoices, log out after inactivity. They are the system's verbs.
    <em>Non-functional</em> requirements (quality attributes) state how well it does
    everything: latency, throughput, availability, security, usability, accessibility,
    maintainability, portability. They are adverbs and adjectives — and they constrain
    every function at once rather than attaching to any single one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second kind is where projects actually die, for two structural reasons. First,
    quality attributes are invisible until they break: no demonstration ever fails on p95
    latency or password storage, so they generate no demo momentum and are quietly deferred
    — until launch week. Second, they interact and compete: adding encryption costs latency;
    maximising availability complicates consistency; hardening security degrades usability.
    These are genuine trade-offs with no free lunch, and the engineering response is to make
    them explicit and quantified: "the service survives the loss of one data centre with
    under 60 seconds of interruption" is a real requirement with a real architecture behind
    it; "the system should be reliable" is a greeting card.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "non-functional" does not mean optional or secondary.
    The name is unfortunate — these requirements are frequently the binding constraints on
    the entire design. A payments system with a perfect feature set that leaks card numbers
    has failed completely. Some methodologists prefer "quality attribute requirements"
    precisely because the standard name undersells them.
  </p>

  <h2>Specification and validation</h2>
  <p>
    Specification is writing requirements down in a form the team can build and test
    against, and the field has converged on a spectrum rather than a single format. At one
    end sits the classic Software Requirements Specification: numbered, unique, traceable
    statements ("REQ-104: The system shall reject a duplicate invoice number within the same
    fiscal period"), beloved of regulated industries because every clause can map to a test.
    At the other end sits the agile <em>user story</em>: "As a customer, I want a refund
    button so that I can return damaged goods without calling support," made rigorous by its
    <em>acceptance criteria</em> — the concrete conditions under which the story is done.
    The story format trades completeness for adaptability; that trade works when the team
    can talk to users continuously, and fails silently when it cannot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever the format, the writing rules are the same, and they exist to defeat specific
    failure modes. Requirements should be <em>atomic</em> — one requirement, one test:
    "the system shall enter marks and calculate averages quickly" bundles a function, a
    calculation and an undefined quality into one untestable sentence, and should be three
    requirements. They should be <em>singular</em> ("shall" for binding statements, "should"
    for goals, never "may" where someone might read permission). They should name their
    trigger and their result precisely enough that two engineers implement them identically.
    Ambiguity is the enemy, and it hides in innocent words: "fast", "user-friendly",
    "support", "handle", "etc." A useful review drill is to circle every adjective and
    adverb in a specification and demand a number or an example for each — most drafts do
    not survive the exercise, which is exactly the point.
  </p>
  <p>
    Validation — checking that the specified requirements are the <em>right</em> ones — then
    runs the artefacts back past reality. Reviews walk stakeholders through the document
    while it is still prose, when objections cost sentences instead of sprints. Acceptance
    tests make each requirement executable. Prototypes let users point at what is wrong
    rather than failing to imagine what is missing. The distinction to keep sharp is
    validation versus verification: validation asks "are we building the right product?",
    verification asks "are we building the product right?" Requirements defects live
    entirely in the first question, which is why testing the final build can never catch
    them — the build faithfully implements the wrong thing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="defect cost grows phase by phase" widget="plotter" config={costCfg} />

  <p>
    The economics justify all of this ceremony. Barry Boehm's studies in the 1970s and 1980s,
    replicated in industry ever since, found that the cost of fixing a requirements defect
    grows by roughly an order of magnitude per phase: a misunderstanding settled in a
    workshop costs a conversation; found in integration it costs weeks; found in production
    it costs releases, data migrations, emergency patches and — hardest to restore — trust.
    The plotted curve (relative repair cost against the phase where the defect is caught) is
    the single most quoted figure in software economics and the entire business case for
    requirements engineering: cheap conversations, held early and on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Mature practice treats requirements as managed artefacts with lineage. A
    <em>traceability matrix</em> links every requirement to the design elements, code and
    tests that realise it, so that a change upstream reveals exactly what must be revisited
    downstream — and so that an auditor can verify nothing was built without a reason and
    nothing was required without a build. In safety-critical domains this becomes law:
    aviation software (DO-178C), medical devices (IEC 62304) and automotive systems
    (ISO 26262) are unshippable without bidirectional traceability, which is why those
    industries still lead in specification rigour.
  </p>
  <p>
    Change, finally, is managed rather than mourned. A baseline is agreed; a lightweight
    change process receives every proposal; impact analysis uses the traceability matrix to
    price the ripple ("this one sentence touches three components, two tests and a
    compliance claim"); and a decision is made on the record. The alternative is not
    freedom — it is silent drift, where the shipped system diverges from every document
    until nobody, including its authors, can say what it was supposed to do. Even the most
    agile teams practise a version of this, disguised as backlog grooming: the backlog
    <em>is</em> the requirements baseline, and grooming <em>is</em> change control wearing a
    friendlier name. The goal was never a frozen document — it was an honest one, at every
    moment, about what the system promises and why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deeper still are the open problems, and they are genuine research questions. How do you
    elicit requirements for systems whose users do not yet exist? How should quality
    attributes be traded off when stakeholders disagree — multi-attribute methods and
    architecture analysis (ATAM-style scenario walks) exist, but the weights remain
    judgement. How much specification is enough when the domain is so novel that specifying
    fully would take longer than building? And the newest: can language models mine
    elicitation transcripts for contradictions and latent requirements at scale, or do they
    merely automate the production of plausible-sounding ambiguity? The tools will change;
    the invariant underneath will not — software fails at the seams between what was built
    and what was meant, and someone has to own those seams on purpose.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SWEBOK Guide V4.0 (IEEE Computer Society), Requirements Engineering KA; Boehm, <em>Software Engineering Economics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
